\title{zindi: compete on Zindi from R}
\description{
The zindi package wraps the API behind \url{https://zindi.world}: browse
competitions, join them, download their data, submit, wait for your score,
follow the leaderboard, manage your team and read discussions.
}
\section{Credentials}{

Browsing competitions, leaderboards, profiles and discussions needs no
account. Everything else needs an auth token, which zindi looks for in this
order:
\enumerate{
\item a token or username and password given to \code{\link[=zindi_auth]{zindi_auth()}} (or obtained by
\code{\link[=zindi_login]{zindi_login()}}) in the current session;
\item the \code{ZINDI_TOKEN} environment variable;
\item the \code{ZINDI_USERNAME} and \code{ZINDI_PASSWORD} environment variables (zindi
signs in the first time a token is needed);
\item the credentials file saved by \code{\link[=zindi_login]{zindi_login()}}, shared with the Python,
JavaScript and Rust clients (see \code{\link[=zindi_config_dir]{zindi_config_dir()}});
\item in an interactive session, a login prompt.
}
}

\section{Choosing a competition}{

Functions that act on a competition take its id (the slug in its URL, for
example \code{"Crop-Yield-Forecast-Challenge"}). When you leave it out, zindi
uses, in order: the competition chosen with \code{\link[=zindi_use]{zindi_use()}}, the
\code{ZINDI_COMPETITION} environment variable, then the \code{competition} entry of
the nearest \code{zindi.toml} file in the working directory or one of its
parents.
}

\section{Environment variables and options}{

\itemize{
\item \code{ZINDI_API_URL}: API root, \verb{https://api.zindi.world/v1} by default.
\item \code{ZINDI_CONFIG_DIR}: where the credentials file lives.
\item \code{ZINDI_NO_PROMPT}: set to any value to never prompt for credentials.
\item \code{options(zindi.max_tries = 4)}: attempts for reads that fail with HTTP 429
or 5xx, or because the network is down. Uploads and other writes are
never retried.
}
}

\section{Errors}{

Errors raised by zindi inherit from the class \code{zindi_error}, plus one of
\code{zindi_auth_error}, \code{zindi_not_found}, \code{zindi_rate_limit},
\code{zindi_already_joined}, \code{zindi_secret_code_required} or \code{zindi_api_error}
for errors returned by the API. Their \code{status_code} and \code{errors} fields hold
the HTTP status and the raw error object.
}

